    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Reopening works, and 3 of the 4 tests in `bin/e2e` pass: the main \"reopen period\" test and scenarios 2 and 3. **Scenario 1 still fails**, at a step that has nothing to do with periods, and I left it alone because the only way to pass it would make the app show the wrong grade.\n\n**What I changed**\n- **Data:** `Period.reopen()` marks the period open again and clears its frozen total. No migration was needed: the existing `closed` and `closed_total` columns already cover it.\n- **Server:** I added `POST /api/sheets/{sheetId}/periods/{periodId}/reopen`. In `PeriodService.reopen`, anyone who isn't a steward is refused (403), and so is a period that isn't closed (409). A refusal writes nothing to the store or the audit file. A successful reopen writes `PERIOD_REOPENED sheet=<id> by=<user> period=<name>`.\n- **Front end:** each period row now has a `period-reopen` button, and a refusal shows in `period-error`. The button is always shown, because the test clicks it on a period that is already open and expects an error. Once a period is open, its due-total goes back to the live figure, with bound claims counted at their bound figure. In the test that gives 11.00.\n\n**Why scenario 1 fails**\nThe test changes Cam's grade to B starting 2026-03-28, while the sheet's date is 2026-03-20. The `editSave` helper in `e2e/support/ui.ts` then waits for `person-grade` to read \"B\". The app shows the grade in force today, which is still C. The rest of the same test expects exactly that: `person-grade` reads \"C\" (with the date then 2026-03-25), and the audit line is `PERSON_EDITED \u2026 grade=C`. No correct behaviour satisfies both the helper and those later checks, and scenario 1 involves no periods at all, so the reopen change doesn't cause the failure.\n\nPassing it would mean showing \"B\" briefly after saving and \"C\" after a reload. I didn't do that. Since I'm not allowed to edit the test or the helper, fixing it is up to whoever owns them.",
